1
00:00:01,130 --> 00:00:05,100
Here's a sample timeline we
built of ancient Mesopotamia,

2
00:00:05,150 --> 00:00:10,310
the land between the Tigris and the
Euphrates. It runs from the first farming

3
00:00:10,360 --> 00:00:17,170
villages, about seven thousand BCE, to
the fall of Babylon to Cyrus in 539,

4
00:00:17,220 --> 00:00:21,220
and Alexander's death
there two centuries later.

5
00:00:21,270 --> 00:00:25,620
Periods, empires and reigns
are bands; inventions,

6
00:00:25,670 --> 00:00:29,756
writings, laws and battles are
moments, each linked to its sources.

7
00:00:31,446 --> 00:00:35,696
Zoom into the fourth millennium
and the first cities appear.

8
00:00:35,746 --> 00:00:40,586
In the Uruk period come cylinder
seals, a temple on a high platform,

9
00:00:40,636 --> 00:00:45,386
wagons on wheels, and
around 3300 BCE, writing:

10
00:00:45,436 --> 00:00:51,046
clay tablets that began as bookkeeping.
The same accounts already count in

11
00:00:51,096 --> 00:00:54,831
sixties, which is why an hour
still has sixty minutes.

12
00:00:56,541 --> 00:01:00,881
Six centuries on, Sumer is a
patchwork of rival city-states,

13
00:01:00,931 --> 00:01:06,291
Lagash fighting Umma over fields and
water. Then Sargon of Akkad conquers them

14
00:01:06,341 --> 00:01:12,421
all: the first empire. His daughter
Enheduanna may be the first author we know

15
00:01:12,471 --> 00:01:16,481
by name. Akkad falls, and
Ur rises in its place,

16
00:01:16,531 --> 00:01:18,455
with its great ziggurat.

17
00:01:20,005 --> 00:01:24,445
Pull out one thread. Separate
by tags, choose law,

18
00:01:24,495 --> 00:01:27,945
and centuries of law-making
rise into a track of their own:

19
00:01:27,995 --> 00:01:32,525
Urukagina's reforms, Ur-Nammu's
code, the oldest that survives,

20
00:01:32,575 --> 00:01:36,846
the laws of Lipit-Ishtar and
Eshnunna, and then Hammurabi's.

21
00:01:38,386 --> 00:01:42,456
Open the Code of Hammurabi to read it:
two hundred and eighty-two laws on a

22
00:01:42,506 --> 00:01:47,456
basalt stele, where that comes
from, and what it's linked to.

23
00:01:47,506 --> 00:01:53,696
Six centuries later an Elamite king
carried the stele off to Susa as a trophy.

24
00:01:53,746 --> 00:01:56,130
Follow the link, and there it is.

25
00:01:57,830 --> 00:02:02,410
How sure are these dates?
Before about 1500 BCE,

26
00:02:02,460 --> 00:02:06,790
not very, and every moment
says how its date is known.

27
00:02:06,840 --> 00:02:11,100
The Hittite sack of Babylon is
1595 in the middle chronology,

28
00:02:11,150 --> 00:02:14,970
used here; other reckonings
move it by decades,

29
00:02:15,020 --> 00:02:20,570
because they hang on a tablet of Venus
observations that fits the sky more than

30
00:02:20,620 --> 00:02:27,480
one way. Assyria is firmer: an eclipse
noted in its list of years has been dated

31
00:02:27,530 --> 00:02:29,910
to June 763 BCE.

32
00:02:31,460 --> 00:02:36,670
Zoom into the first millennium and Assyria's
kings follow one another as bands:

33
00:02:36,720 --> 00:02:39,770
Ashurnasirpal and his
new capital at Kalhu,

34
00:02:39,820 --> 00:02:43,220
Tiglath-Pileser, Sargon
the Second, Sennacherib,

35
00:02:43,270 --> 00:02:47,470
who besieged Lachish and destroyed
Babylon, and Ashurbanipal,

36
00:02:47,520 --> 00:02:51,350
whose library kept the
best copies of Gilgamesh.

37
00:02:51,400 --> 00:02:56,159
In 612, Nineveh falls to the
Babylonians and the Medes.

38
00:02:57,889 --> 00:03:02,529
Babylon takes Assyria's place.
Nebuchadnezzar takes Jerusalem,

39
00:03:02,579 --> 00:03:08,609
on a day a Babylonian chronicle
records, and builds the Ishtar Gate.

40
00:03:08,659 --> 00:03:14,099
Its last king, Nabonidus, dug for the
past: at Sippar he found an inscription of

41
00:03:14,149 --> 00:03:20,639
Naram-Sin, and said it was 3,200 years
old. On this line you can see it was

42
00:03:20,689 --> 00:03:23,028
about 1,700.

43
00:03:24,578 --> 00:03:30,398
And in 539 the line comes down
to days. The Battle of Opis,

44
00:03:30,448 --> 00:03:33,908
Sippar's surrender, and then,
on the twelfth of October,

45
00:03:33,958 --> 00:03:39,208
Babylon itself falls; Cyrus
rides in seventeen days later.

46
00:03:39,258 --> 00:03:42,488
The ruler counts in the
proleptic Gregorian calendar,

47
00:03:42,538 --> 00:03:47,628
so it says the sixth; each moment keeps
the Julian date historians use beside

48
00:03:47,678 --> 00:03:48,415
it.

49
00:03:50,115 --> 00:03:55,445
Two centuries later, Alexander
dies in Nebuchadnezzar's palace.

50
00:03:55,495 --> 00:03:58,785
Six and a half thousand years,
from villages to empires,

51
00:03:58,835 --> 00:04:04,355
on one line you can read, zoom and
follow. Open it at timescale.info,

52
00:04:04,405 --> 00:04:06,209
and build your own.
